\subsection{Per-Layer Action Decoding Across Training Stages}
\label{sec:action_r2_threecond}

Figure~\ref{fig:action_r2_threecond} compares per-layer action probe $R^2$ across three training stages (untrained, Llama-2 base, VLA-trained) using 50 demonstrations per task and the same probe architecture.
All three conditions share L0 representations (the visual projector output), confirming that subsequent differences arise from Transformer processing rather than input variation.
In mid-layers (L2--L9), $R^2$ follows a consistent gradient (trained $>$ Llama-2 base $>$ untrained), suggesting that VLA fine-tuning builds on language pretraining to enhance action encoding; the trained peak shifts from L4 (Llama-2 base) to L8, consistent with deeper mid-layer specialization.
The untrained model exhibits catastrophic negative $R^2$ at L10--L11, indicating that randomly initialized Transformer layers produce destructive interference on action-relevant structure.
These patterns are consistent with the entry-layer routing specialization reported in \S\ref{sec:learned_routing}; however, this analysis uses a single seed without confidence intervals and should be interpreted as a supplementary observation.

